-- An annotation written above a later clause of a rule written as clauses is
-- not used: the rule takes its @desc, @export and @nlg from above its first
-- clause. Each one draws a warning at the annotation (refutation of
-- legalese/l4-ide#545's review, round 3). Overloads written one after
-- another use theirs and draw none (pattern-matching-overloads-own-annotations.l4).

-- A group over numbers, with one of each above its second, third and fourth
-- clauses. The @desc above the first clause is the rule's own.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A STRING
@desc how many there are
DECIDE `how many` 0 IS "none"
@desc not used
DECIDE `how many` 1 IS "one"
@export not used either
DECIDE `how many` 2 IS "two"
@nlg many things
DECIDE `how many` other IS "many"

#EVAL `how many` 2   -- "two"

-- A group over the values of a type, whose patterns are bare names, as the
-- patterns of overloads are; this one stays a group, so its @export is not
-- used and the rule is not published.
DECLARE Colour IS ONE OF Red, Green

GIVEN c IS A Colour
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `colour code` Red IS 1
@export the green code
DECIDE `colour code` Green IS 2

#EVAL `colour code` Green   -- 2
